import{Q as e,X as t,ft as n,n as r}from"./chunks/framework.DPI0H_hR.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ZIP Archives as Virtual File Systems","description":"","frontmatter":{"id":"proposal.0015","title":"ZIP Archives as Virtual File Systems","authors":["Mark"],"status":"draft","pull-request":"https://github.com/bento-js/bento/pull/280","issues":[],"supersedes":[]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"proposals/0015-zip-archives-as-virtual-file-systems.md","filePath":"proposals/0015-zip-archives-as-virtual-file-systems.md"}`),a={name:`proposals/0015-zip-archives-as-virtual-file-systems.md`};function o(r,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="zip-archives-as-virtual-file-systems" tabindex="-1">ZIP Archives as Virtual File Systems <a class="header-anchor" href="#zip-archives-as-virtual-file-systems" aria-label="Permalink to “ZIP Archives as Virtual File Systems”">​</a></h1><h2 id="summary" tabindex="-1">Summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#summary" aria-label="Permalink to “Summary”">​</a></h2><p>Bento serves ZIP archives as virtual file systems, as Node 26.13.1 does. This brings <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code>, the <code>node:zlib</code> classes it is built from (<code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, and <code>ZipEntry</code>), a Rust <code>ZipProvider</code>, and archive sources for <code>--vfs-load</code> and <code>start_vfs_load</code>. Without them, a ZIP-packaged program needs extracting before it can run.</p><h2 id="motivation" tabindex="-1">Motivation <a class="header-anchor" href="#motivation" aria-label="Permalink to “Motivation”">​</a></h2><p>A ZIP archive is the common way to carry a program or its assets as one file. Node 26 serves one directly, without extraction, in three ways:</p><ul><li><code>node --vfs-load=app.zip</code> runs a program from an archive;</li><li><code>new vfs.ZipProvider(archive)</code> mounts an archive for <code>node:fs</code>, <code>require</code>, and <code>import</code>;</li><li>since v26.8, <code>node:zlib</code> reads, edits, and writes archives through <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, and <code>ZipEntry</code>.</li></ul><p>Tests read fixtures from archives this way, tools inspect and patch them, and applications ship their assets in one.</p><p>Bento supports none of it. Proposal 0010 (<a href="https://github.com/bento-js/bento/pull/270" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PR #270</a>) gives Bento virtual file systems but leaves <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> throwing <code>NotCapable</code>. Proposal 0014 (<a href="https://github.com/bento-js/bento/pull/279" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PR #279</a>) adds <code>--vfs-load</code> for directories, but treats archive files as invalid sources. Bento&#39;s <code>node:zlib</code>, from <code>deno_node</code> 0.197.0, has no ZIP classes at all.</p><p>An agent harness has the same need from Rust: hand the guest a project snapshot as one archive and read back what it changed, without extracting it to disk.</p><h2 id="domain-grounding" tabindex="-1">Domain grounding <a class="header-anchor" href="#domain-grounding" aria-label="Permalink to “Domain grounding”">​</a></h2><p>Observations below were made on macOS arm64 on 2026-11-27 with Node v26.13.1, with <code>--experimental-vfs</code> for <code>node:vfs</code> and <code>--vfs-load</code>, using probe scripts that exercised each claim directly.</p><h3 id="established-context" tabindex="-1">Established context <a class="header-anchor" href="#established-context" aria-label="Permalink to “Established context”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>The ZIP format.</strong> An archive is a sequence of member records (a local header followed by content). After them come a central directory listing every member and an end-of-central-directory record. (<a href="https://pkware.cachefly.net/webdocs/casestudies/APPNOTE.TXT" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PKWARE APPNOTE</a>) <ul><li>Readers locate the end record from the tail and treat the central directory as authoritative.</li><li>Zip64 structures extend sizes, offsets, and counts beyond 32 and 16 bits.</li><li>Common compression methods are <code>0</code> (stored), <code>8</code> (deflate), and <code>93</code> (Zstandard).</li><li>Names are bytes: UTF-8 when general-purpose bit 11 is set or a Unicode-path extra field is present, and historically CP437 otherwise.</li><li>A member&#39;s name can contain <code>..</code> or a leading <code>/</code>, the &quot;Zip Slip&quot; hazard for extractors. A small archive can declare enormous members, the &quot;zip bomb&quot;.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Node&#39;s ZIP archive API.</strong> Added to <code>node:zlib</code> in v26.8.0 at Stability 1.0, Early development. &quot;Using any part of it... emits an experimental warning the first time.&quot; Observed: <code>ExperimentalWarning: The zlib ZIP archive API is an experimental feature and might change at any time</code>. Observed exports: <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipEntry</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, <code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, <code>zipFiles</code>, <code>getMaxZipContentSize</code>, and <code>setMaxZipContentSize</code>. (<a href="https://nodejs.org/api/zlib.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Node <code>node:zlib</code></a>)</li><li><strong><code>ZipBuffer</code></strong> is &quot;an in-memory, zero-copy view over the entries of a ZIP archive already held in a Buffer, TypedArray, DataView, or ArrayBuffer&quot;. <ul><li>Edits are not written into the source; <code>toBuffer()</code> and <code>toBufferSync()</code> serialize the current entries into a fresh archive, switching to Zip64 when needed. Its <code>writable</code> is always <code>true</code>.</li><li><code>add()</code>, <code>addSync()</code>, and <code>addEntry()</code> replace an entry of the same name, and <code>delete()</code> drops one.</li><li><code>get()</code> throws <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND</code>, and <code>comment</code> round-trips.</li><li>Construction throws <code>ERR_ZIP_INVALID_ARCHIVE</code> or <code>ERR_ZIP_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE</code> for a malformed or unsupported archive. Observed for an empty buffer: <code>invalid ZIP archive: no end of central directory record found</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>ZipFile</code></strong> is &quot;a random-access view over the entries of a ZIP archive on disk&quot;. Only the tail and the central directory are read up front, and content is read lazily. <ul><li><code>ZipFile.open(path, { writable: true })</code> opens it read-write. <code>add</code> and <code>addEntry</code> then append a member where the central directory began and rewrite the directory after it, while <code>delete</code> rewrites only the directory.</li><li>These edits are not crash-atomic. Replaced and deleted members remain as dead space until <code>compact()</code> or <code>compactSync()</code> writes a fresh archive elsewhere.</li><li>Writing to an archive opened read-only throws <code>ERR_ZIP_NOT_WRITABLE</code>, and an oversized central directory throws <code>ERR_ZIP_ARCHIVE_TOO_LARGE</code>.</li><li>Every method has a synchronous form. A synchronous method called while an asynchronous edit is unsettled throws <code>ERR_INVALID_STATE</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>ZipEntry</code></strong> is one member. <ul><li><code>ZipEntry.create()</code>, <code>createSync()</code>, <code>createStream()</code>, and <code>createSymlink()</code> build entries. The methods are <code>&#39;deflate&#39;</code> (default), <code>&#39;store&#39;</code>, or <code>&#39;zstd&#39;</code>, with stored output when compression would not help, and <code>mode</code> defaults to <code>0o644</code>.</li><li><code>content()</code> and <code>contentSync()</code> verify CRC-32 by default, throwing <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_CORRUPT</code>. They reject a declared size above <code>maxSize</code>, which defaults to <code>zlib.getMaxZipContentSize()</code> (256 MiB), with <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_TOO_LARGE</code>. <code>contentIterator()</code> streams without that default ceiling.</li><li><code>name</code> is returned verbatim and never normalized.</li><li><code>modified</code> prefers NTFS, Info-ZIP extended, or Info-ZIP Unix timestamp extra fields over the 2-second MS-DOS field.</li><li><code>method</code> is the raw number, as in <code>93</code> observed for a <code>&#39;zstd&#39;</code> entry.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>vfs.ZipProvider(source)</code></strong> exposes a <code>ZipBuffer</code> or <code>ZipFile</code> through the VFS API. (<a href="https://nodejs.org/api/vfs.html#class-zipprovider" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Node <code>ZipProvider</code></a>) Observed: <ul><li>Any other argument throws <code>ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE</code>, with <code>The &quot;source&quot; argument must be an instance of ZipBuffer or ZipFile. Received an instance of Buffer</code>.</li><li><code>readdir</code> lists explicit and implicit directories alike; an archive made with <code>zip -D</code>, which has no directory entries, still lists <code>dir</code>, and <code>statSync(&#39;/dir&#39;).isDirectory()</code> is <code>true</code>.</li><li>Stats report <code>dev</code> <code>4085</code>. A file entry recording mode <code>0o644</code> or <code>0</code> reports <code>100644</code>, one recording <code>0o755</code> reports <code>100755</code>, and the root and implicit directories report <code>40755</code>. <code>readonly</code> is <code>false</code> over a <code>ZipBuffer</code>, and <code>supportsSymlinks</code> and <code>supportsWatch</code> are <code>false</code>.</li><li>Over a <code>ZipBuffer</code>, writing <code>/new.txt</code> and unlinking <code>/a.txt</code> show in <code>zipBuffer.toBufferSync()</code>. Renaming works, and <code>symlinkSync</code> throws <code>ERR_METHOD_NOT_IMPLEMENTED</code>.</li><li>Over a read-only <code>ZipFile</code>, writes fail with <code>EROFS</code>. Over a writable <code>ZipFile</code>, a written file is in the archive on disk after <code>closeSync()</code>.</li><li>A file opened for writing &quot;only commits its content (as a new archive entry) when the handle is closed&quot;.</li><li>An archive with an encrypted member constructs, but reading the member throws <code>ERR_ZIP_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE</code> with <code>unsupported ZIP feature: entry &quot;a.txt&quot; is encrypted</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong><code>--vfs-load</code> with an archive.</strong> Observed: <ul><li>A file whose bytes are a ZIP archive is mounted with a <code>ZipProvider</code> whatever its name; <code>app.bin</code> worked.</li><li>The mount is read-only, and a write fails with <code>EROFS</code>.</li><li>A file that is not an archive fails with <code>ERR_VFS_INVALID_TARGET</code>, with <code>&lt;real path&gt; is not a valid --vfs-load source: must be an existing file or directory</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Upstream crates.</strong><ul><li><code>deno_node</code> 0.197.0&#39;s <code>node:zlib</code> polyfill implements streams and convenience functions, including Zstandard, but no ZIP classes.</li><li>Bento&#39;s lockfile already carries <code>flate2</code>, <code>zlib-rs</code>, <code>miniz_oxide</code>, and <code>zstd</code> 0.13.2. It carries no ZIP crate.</li></ul></li></ul><h3 id="relevant-constraints-and-principles" tabindex="-1">Relevant constraints and principles <a class="header-anchor" href="#relevant-constraints-and-principles" aria-label="Permalink to “Relevant constraints and principles”">​</a></h3><ul><li><a href="./../VISION">VISION.md</a>: &quot;Compatibility is behavior, not a list of names.&quot; The API is Node&#39;s, so its errors, edge cases, and safety checks are part of the promise. &quot;Pay only for what the host uses&quot;: a host that needs no archives should not have to link ZIP code.</li><li>Proposal 0010 supplies the <code>VirtualProvider</code> trait, the providers&#39; shared semantics, and the rule that real-disk operations are checked with the permissions of the guest that caused them. Proposal 0014 supplies source choice for <code>--vfs-load</code> and <code>start_vfs_load</code>, which this proposal extends to archives.</li><li><a href="./../policies/0001-prefer-voidzero-crates">policy.0001</a> governs parsing, transformation, resolution, and bundling. Archive reading and writing is none of those, so it does not decide the ZIP dependency.</li></ul><h3 id="quality-bar" tabindex="-1">Quality bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#quality-bar" aria-label="Permalink to “Quality bar”">​</a></h3><ul><li>For every <code>ZipProvider</code>, <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, and <code>ZipEntry</code> call this design covers, Bento produces Node v26.13.1&#39;s observable result: entries, contents, metadata, error <code>code</code>, and message. The only exceptions are the differences listed in <a href="#where-bento-differs-from-node">Where Bento differs from Node</a>.</li><li>Archives Bento writes are read correctly by Node and by <code>unzip</code>, and archives they write are read correctly by Bento.</li><li>No member content is allocated beyond the declared-size ceiling before the check that rejects it.</li></ul><h3 id="remaining-uncertainty" tabindex="-1">Remaining uncertainty <a class="header-anchor" href="#remaining-uncertainty" aria-label="Permalink to “Remaining uncertainty”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The ZIP archive API is at Stability 1.0, Early development, so later Node releases may change it. Bento pins to Node v26.13.1.</li><li>Whether the candidate ZIP crate supports Node&#39;s in-place append to a writable archive directly, or Bento must write that path itself, is an implementation question that does not change the behavior below.</li></ul><h2 id="existing-baseline" tabindex="-1">Existing baseline <a class="header-anchor" href="#existing-baseline" aria-label="Permalink to “Existing baseline”">​</a></h2><p>Bento has no ZIP support. <code>node:zlib</code> is <code>deno_node</code>&#39;s polyfill, and proposal 0010 makes <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> throw <code>NotCapable</code>. Proposal 0014 rejects every file given as a <code>--vfs-load</code> source.</p><p>Worth keeping:</p><ul><li><code>deno_node</code>&#39;s existing <code>node:zlib</code> streams and functions stay as they are. The ZIP classes are added beside them, the way proposal 0010 adds <code>node:vfs</code>.</li><li>The deflate and Zstandard crates already in the lockfile are reused.</li></ul><h2 id="proposed-solution" tabindex="-1">Proposed solution <a class="header-anchor" href="#proposed-solution" aria-label="Permalink to “Proposed solution”">​</a></h2><p><code>bento_vfs</code> gains a ZIP module, behind a <code>zip</code> cargo feature enabled by default. It reads and writes archives in memory and on disk with Node&#39;s semantics, and implements proposal 0010&#39;s <code>VirtualProvider</code> over them.</p><p><code>node:zlib</code> gains <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, and <code>ZipEntry</code> as handles over that module, and <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> accepts them. Proposal 0014&#39;s source choice recognizes archive files by their bytes and mounts them read-only.</p><div class="language-text"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>bento run --vfs-load=app.zip → runs app.zip&#39;s package.json &quot;main&quot; from /dev/null/vfs/0; writes through the mount fail with EROFS</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>new vfs.ZipProvider(new zlib.ZipBuffer(bytes)) → a writable VFS whose edits appear in zipBuffer.toBufferSync()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>new vfs.ZipProvider(zlib.ZipFile.openSync(&#39;assets.zip&#39;)) → a read-only VFS; writes fail with EROFS</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language-rust"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">rust</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">let</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> snapshot </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;"> ZipProvider</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">from_bytes</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">(</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">std</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">fs</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">read</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">(</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;project.zip&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">)</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">?</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">)</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">?</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">config</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">.</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">mounts </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;"> vec!</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">[</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">Arc</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">new</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">(snapshot</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">.</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">clone</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">())];</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#818e99;--shiki-light:#62687b;">// ... run the guest ...</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">std</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">fs</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">::</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">write</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">(</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;project-after.zip&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">, snapshot</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">.</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#B392F0;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">to_bytes</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">()</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">?</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">)</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#F97583;--shiki-light:#c62739;">?</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-light:#24292E;">;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="detailed-design" tabindex="-1">Detailed design <a class="header-anchor" href="#detailed-design" aria-label="Permalink to “Detailed design”">​</a></h2><h3 id="vocabulary" tabindex="-1">Vocabulary <a class="header-anchor" href="#vocabulary" aria-label="Permalink to “Vocabulary”">​</a></h3><ul><li>An <strong>archive</strong> is a ZIP byte sequence.</li><li>An <strong>entry</strong> is one member of an archive: a file, a directory (its name ends in <code>/</code>), or a symbolic link (its Unix mode marks it as one).</li><li>An <strong>in-memory archive</strong> is held in memory and serialized on request; this is Node&#39;s <code>ZipBuffer</code>.</li><li>An <strong>archive file</strong> is read from disk lazily and, when <strong>writable</strong>, edited in place; this is Node&#39;s <code>ZipFile</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="node-zlib-zip-classes" tabindex="-1"><code>node:zlib</code> ZIP classes <a class="header-anchor" href="#node-zlib-zip-classes" aria-label="Permalink to “node:zlib ZIP classes”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>node:zlib</code> gains <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, <code>ZipEntry</code>, <code>getMaxZipContentSize</code>, and <code>setMaxZipContentSize</code>.</li><li>Each member Node 26.13.1 documents for these five behaves as documented and as Domain grounding records. That covers construction, the synchronous and asynchronous methods, iteration order, replacement by name, <code>comment</code>, <code>writable</code>, <code>toBuffer</code>, <code>compact</code>, <code>stream</code>, content verification and ceilings, and <code>ZipEntry</code>&#39;s properties and static methods.</li><li><code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, and <code>zipFiles</code> are not added; see the open questions.</li><li>Supported methods are stored, deflate, and Zstandard. Zip64 archives are read, and Zip64 structures are written whenever a count, size, or offset needs them.</li><li>Names are decoded as Node decodes them: Unicode-path extra field, then UTF-8 by flag or validity, then CP437. They are returned verbatim.</li><li>Unsupported features, such as encryption, raise <code>ERR_ZIP_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE</code> when the affected entry is read, with Node&#39;s message.</li><li>The errors are Node&#39;s: <code>ERR_ZIP_INVALID_ARCHIVE</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_NOT_FOUND</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_TOO_LARGE</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_ENTRY_CORRUPT</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_NOT_WRITABLE</code>, <code>ERR_ZIP_ARCHIVE_TOO_LARGE</code>, and <code>ERR_INVALID_STATE</code>, each with Node&#39;s message.</li><li>Writable archive files are edited in place as Node edits them, including dead space and the lack of crash atomicity, which the guide states.</li><li>Bento emits no <code>ExperimentalWarning</code> for the ZIP archive API, as proposal 0010 decides for <code>node:vfs</code>.</li><li><code>ZipFile.open</code> and <code>openSync</code> are file system operations of the guest on the archive&#39;s real path. They need read permission, and write permission when <code>writable</code> is <code>true</code>, under proposal 0003&#39;s rules. A <code>ZipBuffer</code> touches no file.</li></ul><h3 id="vfs-zipprovider" tabindex="-1"><code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#vfs-zipprovider" aria-label="Permalink to “vfs.ZipProvider”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>new vfs.ZipProvider(source)</code> takes a <code>ZipBuffer</code> or a <code>ZipFile</code>. Anything else throws <code>ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE</code>, with <code>The &quot;source&quot; argument must be an instance of ZipBuffer or ZipFile. Received &lt;description&gt;</code>. This replaces proposal 0010&#39;s <code>NotCapable</code>.</li><li><strong>Directories</strong> are explicit directory entries and every name prefix ending in <code>/</code>. Both are listed by <code>readdir</code>, including recursively, and <code>stat</code> reports them as directories.</li><li><strong>Stats</strong> are proposal 0010&#39;s synthetic stats, with <code>dev</code> <code>4085</code>. A file reports mode <code>0o100000</code> plus the entry&#39;s permission bits, or <code>0o100644</code> when the entry records <code>0</code>. The root and implicit directories report <code>0o40755</code>, and a directory entry reports <code>0o40000</code> plus its permission bits.</li><li><strong>Reads</strong> decompress the entry. An encrypted entry fails with <code>ERR_ZIP_UNSUPPORTED_FEATURE</code>.</li><li><strong>Writes.</strong> A file opened for writing becomes an entry, replacing one of the same name, when its handle closes. <ul><li>Over a <code>ZipBuffer</code>, the entry lives in memory and appears in <code>toBuffer()</code>.</li><li>Over a writable <code>ZipFile</code>, it is appended to the archive on disk.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Other changes.</strong><ul><li><code>mkdir</code> adds a directory entry, and <code>unlink</code> and <code>rmdir</code> delete entries.</li><li><code>rename</code> adds the target and deletes the source.</li><li><code>symlink</code> throws <code>ERR_METHOD_NOT_IMPLEMENTED</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Flags.</strong> <code>readonly</code> is the opposite of the archive&#39;s <code>writable</code>, and writes to a read-only archive fail with <code>EROFS</code>. <code>supportsSymlinks</code> and <code>supportsWatch</code> are <code>false</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="rust-zipprovider" tabindex="-1">Rust <code>ZipProvider</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rust-zipprovider" aria-label="Permalink to “Rust ZipProvider”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>ZipProvider::from_bytes(bytes: Vec&lt;u8&gt;) -&gt; io::Result&lt;ZipProvider&gt;</code> is an in-memory archive, always writable. <code>to_bytes() -&gt; io::Result&lt;Vec&lt;u8&gt;&gt;</code> serializes its current entries.</li><li><code>ZipProvider::open(path, writable: bool) -&gt; io::Result&lt;ZipProvider&gt;</code> opens an archive file. The file closes when the last clone is dropped.</li><li><code>ZipProvider</code> implements proposal 0010&#39;s <code>VirtualProvider</code> with the semantics above. Malformed archives fail with an <code>io::Error</code> of kind <code>InvalidData</code>, which carries Node&#39;s <code>ERR_ZIP_INVALID_ARCHIVE</code> message.</li><li>Clones share state, like proposal 0010&#39;s providers. A <code>ZipProvider</code> works in <code>RuntimeConfig.mounts</code>, in a <code>ComposableProvider</code>, and as a load.</li></ul><h3 id="archive-sources" tabindex="-1">Archive sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#archive-sources" aria-label="Permalink to “Archive sources”">​</a></h3><p>This section extends proposal 0014&#39;s provider choice.</p><ul><li>A regular file whose bytes are a ZIP archive, meaning they contain an end-of-central-directory record, is served by a read-only <code>ZipProvider</code> over the file, whatever its name. Every other file remains an invalid source with proposal 0014&#39;s message.</li><li>Writes through an archive load mount fail with <code>EROFS</code>.</li><li>Under <code>--permission</code>, <code>--allow-fs-read</code> must cover the archive, as for a directory source.</li><li><code>bento run --watch</code> restarts when the archive file changes.</li><li><code>VfsLoad::source(path)</code> applies the same rules, and <code>process.argv[1]</code> is the archive&#39;s real path.</li></ul><h3 id="where-bento-differs-from-node" tabindex="-1">Where Bento differs from Node <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-bento-differs-from-node" aria-label="Permalink to “Where Bento differs from Node”">​</a></h3><p>These are the only intended differences from Node v26.13.1:</p><ul><li><strong>No warning.</strong> Bento prints no <code>ExperimentalWarning</code> for the ZIP archive API.</li><li><strong>Compressed bytes.</strong> Archives Bento writes are valid, and every entry&#39;s content, name, mode, time, and comment round-trip. The compressed bytes need not match Node&#39;s, because the deflate and Zstandard encoders differ.</li><li><strong>Missing helpers.</strong> <code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, and <code>zipFiles</code> are absent, pending the open question.</li></ul><h3 id="documentation" tabindex="-1">Documentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#documentation" aria-label="Permalink to “Documentation”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The virtual-file-system guide from proposal 0010 gains a section on archives. It covers <code>ZipProvider</code>, archive sources for <code>--vfs-load</code>, the read-only archive mount, and the safety notes on verbatim names, declared sizes, and in-place edits.</li><li><a href="./../guides/node-compatibility">Node Compatibility</a> lists the <code>node:zlib</code> ZIP classes.</li></ul><h2 id="compatibility" tabindex="-1">Compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to “Compatibility”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Guest code.</strong> The change is additive: <code>node:zlib</code> gains five names, and <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> stops throwing <code>NotCapable</code>.</li><li><strong>CLI.</strong> <code>--vfs-load</code> accepts archive files that proposal 0014 rejected.</li><li><strong>Dependencies.</strong> <code>ZipProvider</code> and the <code>node:zlib</code> classes depend on proposal 0010. Archive sources depend on proposal 0014 as well.</li><li>Nothing stored needs migrating.</li></ul><h2 id="implications-on-adoption" tabindex="-1">Implications on adoption <a class="header-anchor" href="#implications-on-adoption" aria-label="Permalink to “Implications on adoption”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The workspace gains one dependency for the ZIP format; the <code>zip</code> crate is the candidate. It reuses the deflate and Zstandard crates already locked.</li><li>The <code>zip</code> feature lets embedded builds leave ZIP support out.</li><li>Nothing changes for users who do not use archives, and no version floor changes.</li></ul><h2 id="scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope" aria-label="Permalink to “Scope”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The ZIP module of <code>bento_vfs</code> behind the <code>zip</code> feature, covering reading, writing, in-place append, Zip64, stored, deflate, and Zstandard.</li><li><code>node:zlib</code>&#39;s <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, <code>ZipEntry</code>, <code>getMaxZipContentSize</code>, and <code>setMaxZipContentSize</code>.</li><li><code>vfs.ZipProvider</code> and the Rust <code>ZipProvider</code>.</li><li>Archive sources for <code>--vfs-load</code> and <code>VfsLoad::source</code>, including <code>--watch</code>.</li><li>Tests derived from the Detailed design. Node expectations come from Domain grounding&#39;s observations, and cross-reading tests cover Node and <code>unzip</code>.</li><li>The guide updates and changeset named above.</li></ul><h3 id="out-of-scope" tabindex="-1">Out of scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#out-of-scope" aria-label="Permalink to “Out of scope”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Single executable applications&#39; <code>&quot;vfsArchive&quot;</code>: <a href="https://github.com/bento-js/bento/issues/241" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#241</a>.</li><li><code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, and <code>zipFiles</code>, unless the open question brings them in.</li><li>Encrypted and multi-disk archives, which Node does not support either.</li><li>Extracting archives to disk: Node&#39;s API never writes members out, and neither does Bento&#39;s.</li></ul><h2 id="preview" tabindex="-1">Preview <a class="header-anchor" href="#preview" aria-label="Permalink to “Preview”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Artifact.</strong> The installable <code>bento</code> archive from the pull request&#39;s macOS CI job (<code>bento-macos27-arm64-prompt</code>).</li><li><strong>Exercising it.</strong> The human runs scripts that build, edit, and read archives through <code>node:zlib</code> and mount them with <code>vfs.ZipProvider</code>, and <code>bento run --vfs-load=app.zip</code> against a packaged program. They compare each result with <code>node --experimental-vfs</code> and open Bento&#39;s output archives with <code>unzip</code>.</li><li><strong>Reason.</strong> Bento publishes no packages, and the CLI exercises both the JavaScript API and the archive source path.</li><li><strong>Cleanup.</strong> None beyond the CI artifact.</li></ul><h2 id="policies-and-decisions-checked" tabindex="-1">Policies and decisions checked <a class="header-anchor" href="#policies-and-decisions-checked" aria-label="Permalink to “Policies and decisions checked”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./../policies/0001-prefer-voidzero-crates">policy.0001</a> — Not engaged. Archive handling is not one of its roles, and no Deno or SWC parsing crate is added.</li><li><code>decisions/</code> — No decision records exist yet.</li><li>Proposals 0010 and 0014 (in flight) — Builds on both: on 0010&#39;s provider trait, synthetic stats, and permission rule, and on 0014&#39;s source choice and messages.</li><li>Proposal 0003 (in flight) — <code>ZipFile.open</code> and archive sources use its file permissions.</li><li><a href="./../VISION">VISION.md</a> — Honored: ZIP support is feature-selected, and the behavior is measured against Node&#39;s.</li></ul><h2 id="future-directions" tabindex="-1">Future directions <a class="header-anchor" href="#future-directions" aria-label="Permalink to “Future directions”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A self-contained <code>bento</code> binary (#241) could embed an application archive and mount it as the load mount, as Node&#39;s <code>&quot;vfsArchive&quot;</code> does.</li><li><code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, and <code>zipFiles</code> would complete Node&#39;s ZIP archive API, if not added here.</li></ul><h2 id="alternatives-considered" tabindex="-1">Alternatives considered <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-considered" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives considered”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Ship <code>ZipProvider</code> for <code>--vfs-load</code> and Rust only, without the <code>node:zlib</code> classes.</strong> It is smaller, but <code>new vfs.ZipProvider(...)</code> would still throw, because its argument must be a <code>ZipBuffer</code> or a <code>ZipFile</code>. This is the subject of the open question.</li><li><strong>Extract archives to a temporary directory and serve them with <code>RealFsProvider</code>.</strong> It writes to disk, exposes temporary paths, and does not match Node&#39;s read-only archive mount or its in-place edits.</li><li><strong>Port Node&#39;s JavaScript ZIP implementation.</strong> A Rust host could not use it without running JavaScript, and it would duplicate the provider that archive sources need in Rust.</li><li><strong>A separate <code>bento_zip</code> crate.</strong> A feature inside <code>bento_vfs</code> keeps the provider beside the trait it implements and still lets builds omit it.</li></ul><h2 id="open-questions" tabindex="-1">Open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to “Open questions”">​</a></h2><ul><li>[NEEDS CLARIFICATION: Should this proposal implement Node&#39;s ZIP archive classes as written: <code>ZipBuffer</code>, <code>ZipFile</code>, the <code>ZipEntry</code> they return, and the content-size ceiling? Or should it implement the whole ZIP archive API, adding <code>createZipArchive</code>, <code>createZipArchiveSync</code>, and <code>zipFiles</code>? Or none of the JavaScript API, shipping ZIP only for <code>--vfs-load</code>, <code>start_vfs_load</code>, and Rust while <code>new vfs.ZipProvider(...)</code> keeps throwing <code>NotCapable</code>?]</li></ul>`,63)]])}var s=r(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};